CallManager --> ChatClient : uses (&), listens to call_* signals
CallManager --> AudioInputDevice : uses (&), pcmDataAvailable()\n-> pushCapturedAudio() (issue #70)
CallManager --> AudioOutputDevice : uses (&), PlayoutSink\n-> writeAudio() (issue #70)
CallManager --> CameraDevice : uses (&), frameAvailable()\n-> pushFrame() (issue #72)
CallManager --> ScreenCaptureDevice : uses (&), frameAvailable()\n-> pushFrame() (issue #112)
CallManager *-- WebrtcPeerConnectionFactory : owns (built lazily on joinCall())
CallManager "1" *-- "0..*" WebrtcPeerConnection : owns, one per\nremote participant (mesh)
CallManager *-- CallAudioDeviceModule : owns (issue #64), registered\nas the factory's ADM
CallManager *-- CallVideoTrackSource : owns (issue #72), created lazily\non first enableVideo()
note right of CallManager
  Mesh initiation rule (issue #46): the newly
  joining peer always offers to everyone
  already in call_roster; existing participants
  only ever answer, in response to an incoming
  offer via callSignalReceived() — never on
  callPeerJoined(), which is purely informational.

  PeerConnectionObserver / SetLocal-/
  SetRemoteDescriptionObserverInterface
  callbacks fire on WebRTC's own signaling
  thread; every one hops back to this class's
  own (GUI) thread via QMetaObject::invokeMethod
  before touching any shared state.

  Real microphone/speaker audio (issue #70):
  joinCall() wires AudioInputDevice::pcmDataAvailable()
  into pushCapturedAudio(), and CallAudioDeviceModule's
  PlayoutSink into AudioOutputDevice::writeAudio() —
  both single-owner-at-a-time with the Settings
  dialog's mic-test/test-tone controls, so a call and
  those controls silently take over the same
  QAudioSource/QAudioSink from each other (accepted
  first-pass tradeoff). A null/unselected QAudioDevice
  is handled with callError() rather than being passed
  through — WebRTC's own AudioTransportImpl hard-aborts
  (fatal CHECK) on a 0 channel count instead of failing
  gracefully.

  Camera video (issue #72): the shared video track is
  created once, on the first enableVideo() call, and
  attached to every existing PeerConnection right there
  — negotiateLocal() is called explicitly for each one,
  the same way ensurePeerConnection()/
  onCallRosterReceived() already negotiate the initial
  audio track, rather than reacting to WebRTC's own
  OnRenegotiationNeeded() notification (deliberately left
  as its inherited no-op). Relying on both an explicit
  call AND that notification for the same track change
  raced them against each other during live testing —
  the queued OnRenegotiationNeeded callback fired again
  after the explicit call had already completed the
  negotiation, stacking a second unwanted offer on top
  and corrupting the exchange ("Called in wrong state:
  stable"). A PeerConnection created after video is
  already on picks the track up as part of its own
  initial offer/answer instead (attachVideoTrack(), also
  called from ensurePeerConnection()). Once attached, the
  track is never removed again — enableVideo()/
  disableVideo() only toggle
  VideoTrackInterface::set_enabled(), the same mechanism
  setMuted() uses for audio — because RemoveTrackOrError()
  hit a real fatal assertion inside WebRTC's own
  codec-list handling during live testing
  (media/base/codec_list.cc, "Check failed: present_codec
  == codec").

  Screen share (issue #112) reuses camera video's exact
  track/attach machinery instead of a second track —
  ensureLocalVideoTrack() factors out the "create once,
  attach to every peer" block enableVideo() used to do
  inline. enableVideo()/enableScreenShare() are mutually
  exclusive (enabling one disables the other first) and
  just point the one shared CallVideoTrackSource at a
  different frame source (camera_ vs screenCapture_),
  flipping is_screencast() via setIsScreencast() to
  match. Simpler than a second track (no extra
  renegotiation/attach bookkeeping per peer), at the cost
  of not sending both at once — accepted first-pass scope
  limit.

  Receiving remote video (issue #91) mirrors the sending
  side: PeerObserver::OnTrack() -> handleRemoteTrack()
  attaches a RemoteVideoSink (webrtc::VideoSinkInterface)
  to the peer's incoming video track, guarded by
  PeerConnectionEntry::remoteVideoSink so it only happens
  once. RemoteVideoSink::OnFrame() converts I420 back to
  QImage via libyuv::I420ToARGB — the reverse of
  CallVideoTrackSource's ARGBToI420 — and hops to the GUI
  thread via QMetaObject::invokeMethod before emitting
  remoteVideoFrameReceived(), since OnFrame() fires on a
  WebRTC decode thread, not the GUI thread.
end note

class ChatRestClient {
  +createCommunity(token: QString, name: QString)
  +listCommunities(token: QString)
  +joinCommunity(token: QString, communityId: qint64)
  +createChannel(token: QString, communityId: qint64, name: QString)
  +listChannels(token: QString, communityId: qint64)
  +listMessages(token: QString, channelId: qint64, limit: int, beforeId: qint64)
  +promoteModerator(token: QString, communityId: qint64, targetLogin: QString)
  +demoteModerator(token: QString, communityId: qint64, targetLogin: QString)
  +listModerators(token: QString, communityId: qint64)
  +uploadAttachment(token: QString, channelId: qint64, filename: QString,\n    contentType: QString, data: QByteArray)
  +downloadAttachment(token: QString, attachmentId: qint64)
  .. signals ..
  +communityCreated(id: qint64, name: QString)
  +communitiesListed(communities: QList<ChatItem>)
  +communityJoined(communityId: qint64)
  +channelCreated(id: qint64, name: QString)
  +channelsListed(channels: QList<ChatItem>)
  +messagesListed(channelId: qint64, messages: QList<ChatMessageInfo>)
  +moderatorPromoted(communityId: qint64, login: QString)
  +moderatorDemoted(communityId: qint64, login: QString)
  +moderatorsListed(communityId: qint64, logins: QStringList)
  +attachmentUploaded(id: qint64, filename: QString)
  +attachmentDownloaded(attachmentId: qint64, data: QByteArray)
  +errorOccurred(message: QString)
}
ChatRestClient *-- QNetworkAccessManager : owns
ChatRestClient ..> ChatItem : emits lists of
ChatRestClient ..> ChatMessageInfo : emits pages of (issue #100)
ChatRestClient ..> "chat-service" : REST (Authorization: Bearer <token>)

note right of ChatClient
  Async only — every call returns
  immediately, result comes back
  via signals. First frame after
  connecting authenticates and
  subscribes to a channel; every
  frame after that is a chat message.
end note

note right of ChatRestClient
  Separate from ChatClient on purpose:
  CRUD/management (this class) vs. the
  live message stream (ChatClient) are
  different responsibilities.
end note

@enduml
